\section{Evaluation}\label{sec:evaluation}

To demonstrate that the \texttt{spec!}/\texttt{\#[when]} macro system
introduces no runtime overhead while improving code maintainability,
we conduct a four-pillar empirical evaluation covering
\emph{runtime performance}, \emph{compilation overhead},
\emph{binary size}, and \emph{source-code conciseness}.

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Benchmark Design}\label{sec:bench-design}

We construct \textbf{33 paired micro-benchmarks}.
Each pair consists of a file that uses our \texttt{spec!} and
\texttt{\#[when]} macros and a corresponding file that implements the
same operation using only standard Rust techniques.
The 33 examples are organised into three groups:

\paragraph{Type specialization (ex01--ex20).}
Twenty examples target concrete-type dispatch on common data-processing
kernels---saturating arithmetic, bit-counting, summation, serialisation,
hashing, sorting, searching, formatting, FMA, and dot-product---with
specialisations for primitive types such as \texttt{u8}, \texttt{i32},
\texttt{f64}, and \texttt{String}.

\paragraph{Advanced pattern specialization (ex21--ex27).}
Seven examples exercise the richer features of the macro system:
lifetime specialization (\texttt{\&'static} vs.\ \texttt{\&'a}),
higher-ranked trait bounds (\texttt{for<'a> fn(\&'a T) -> T}),
cross-generic repeating constraints (\texttt{U = Vec<T>}),
wildcard and complex type patterns (\texttt{Vec<\_>},
\texttt{(i32, \_)}, \texttt{fn(\&u8) -> \&u8}),
conditional combinators (\texttt{any}, \texttt{all}, \texttt{not}),
\texttt{Fn} trait-bound dispatch, and multi-receiver specialization
(same trait across distinct self types).

\paragraph{Stress-test and composite patterns (ex28--ex33).}
Six examples probe corner cases and scalability:
\texttt{T: 'static} lifetime bounds,
\texttt{\&dyn Display} type patterns,
combined trait bounds (\texttt{T: Copy + Eq}),
ten simultaneous \texttt{\#[when]} clauses on a single trait,
tuple-type specialization (\texttt{(i32,i32)}, \texttt{(f64,f64)}),
and nested generic types (\texttt{Vec<Vec<u8>{}>}, \texttt{Vec<String>}).

\medskip
\noindent
For each example the \emph{without-macros} file provides up to five
alternative implementations of the same operation:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Generic fallback} --- the default trait implementation
        with no specialization;
  \item \textbf{Enum dispatch} --- a hand-written enum whose match arms
        select the specialised code path;
  \item \textbf{Runtime \texttt{TypeId}} --- branching on
        \texttt{std::any::TypeId} at runtime;
  \item \textbf{Dynamic dispatch} --- a \texttt{\&dyn Trait} object
        with virtual calls;
  \item \textbf{Manual specialization} --- a hand-monomorphised free
        function (best-case baseline).
\end{enumerate}

% ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\subsection{Methodology}\label{sec:methodology}

All benchmarks are implemented inside a dedicated Cargo crate and
executed with Criterion~0.5 on a single machine.
Each benchmark group iterates over five input sizes
($n \in \{256,\; 1{,}024,\; 4{,}096,\; 16{,}384,\; 65{,}536\}$)
to measure scaling behaviour.
Functions under test are annotated \texttt{\#[inline(never)]} to
prevent the compiler from eliding the dispatch mechanism and
\texttt{std::hint::black\_box} is used to defeat dead-code
elimination.

\paragraph{Runtime performance.}
Criterion reports the median wall-clock time per iteration with
bootstrap confidence intervals.
From these we compute the \emph{speed-up factor}
$S = t_{\text{alt}} / t_{\text{spec}}$
of \texttt{spec!} relative to each alternative at the largest
input size.

\paragraph{Compilation overhead.}
We measure clean-build time of the benchmark crate with and without the
macro system, averaged over five iterations using high-resolution
monotonic timestamps.

\paragraph{Binary size.}
The bench binary is compiled in release mode; we record both the
unstripped and stripped sizes.

\paragraph{Source-code conciseness.}
For each pair we count the number of non-empty, non-comment physical
lines.
The ratio
$R = \text{LOC}_{\text{without}} / \text{LOC}_{\text{with}}$
quantifies the boilerplate reduction provided by the macro system.

