\subsection{Expected Outcomes}\label{sec:expected}

Because \texttt{spec!} resolves to a direct, monomorphised function
call at compile time---with no vtable indirection, no branch, and no
\texttt{TypeId} comparison---we expect:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Runtime:} \texttt{spec!} matches or outperforms all
        alternatives on every example, with the largest gains over
        dynamic dispatch and runtime \texttt{TypeId} checks;
  \item \textbf{Build time:} a small, constant increase due to
        procedural-macro expansion and the \texttt{handle\_order} build
        script;
  \item \textbf{Binary size:} comparable to manually monomorphised
        code, since the macro generates the same trait impls a
        programmer would write by hand;
  \item \textbf{Conciseness:} $R > 1$ for all 33 pairs, since the
        \texttt{\#[when]} annotations replace hand-written enum
        variants, match arms, and boilerplate trait-object wrappers.
\end{itemize}